<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diogo de Azambuja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1432–1518</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diogo de Azambuja was a Portuguese captain and nobleman sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. In 1482, after negotiating with a local ruler remembered as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caramansa">Caramansa</a>, he built the fortress of São Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>, the first permanent European stronghold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>. He served as its first captain, and the fort became the centre of the Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> on the coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diogo-de-azambuja.jpg" alt="Diogo de Azambuja" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Template:Domingos Teixeira · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
